19:T52f,## Too many doors

To do one simple task, a client often has to know a whole **subsystem** of classes and call them in exactly the right order. Want to watch a movie? Turn on the projector, dim the lights, then start the player, miss a step or reorder them and it breaks. The client is now **coupled** to every internal class, and anyone using it has a steep learning curve.

## One simple front door

A **Facade** is a single, high-level class that wraps the subsystem and offers a few easy operations. The client talks only to the facade; the facade does the dance behind the scenes:

```
class TheaterFacade {
    void watchMovie() {
        projector.on();
        lights.dim();
        player.play();
    }
}
```

## What it does, and doesn't, do

A facade **doesn't add behaviour** and it **doesn't hide the subsystem**, advanced clients can still reach in and use the classes directly. It simply provides a convenient default path.

- **Less coupling**, the client depends on one class, not many.
- **Gentler learning curve**, `watchMovie()` instead of a five-step ritual.
- **Freedom to change**, the subsystem can be rewired behind the facade without touching callers.

> The catch: a facade can grow into a **god object** if you keep piling responsibilities on it. Keep it thin, it orchestrates, it doesn't implement.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-structural","activeLesson":"facade"}],["$","$L18","facade",{"lesson":{"id":"facade","patternId":"lld-structural","title":"Facade","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Intro","statement":"Give a complex subsystem ONE simple front door, a single class with easy methods that orchestrate all the messy internals for you.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Netflix"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"Wiring it all yourself","code":["projector.on();","lights.dim();","player.play();   // you must know every class, in the right order"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"To watch a movie YOU talk to every subsystem class directly: Projector, Lights, Player. You depend on all three.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"client","name":"You","x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"red"},{"id":"proj","name":"Projector","methods":["+ on()"],"x":0,"y":1.5},{"id":"lights","name":"Lights","methods":["+ dim()"],"x":1,"y":1.5},{"id":"player","name":"Player","methods":["+ play()"],"x":2,"y":1.5}],"relations":[{"id":"b1","from":"client","to":"proj","kind":"depend"},{"id":"b2","from":"client","to":"lights","kind":"depend"},{"id":"b3","from":"client","to":"player","kind":"depend"}],"shownClassIds":["client","proj","lights","player"],"shownRelIds":["b1","b2","b3"],"activeIds":["client"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":3,"narration":"And you must call them in the right order. Tight coupling and a fiddly ritual, easy to get wrong, painful to change.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["client","proj","lights","player"],"shownRelIds":["b1","b2","b3"],"activeIds":["proj","lights","player"